    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since 2018, TRIANGLE in ASEAN has advocated to Thailand&#x2019;s Labour Ministry for increased domestic workers protection through revision of the Ministerial Regulation. The program engaged in government dialogues and supported domestic workers&apos; advocacy for social security expansion, including for migrant workers. TRIANGLE in ASEAN provided legal recommendations and supported regulation drafting to ensure alignment with international labour standards. In May 2024, Thailand approved the revised 2012 Ministerial Regulation on Domestic Work, improving accessibility to basic work rights and social security. The revised regulation includes provisions for minimum wage, maternity leave, and regular working hours for domestic workers. This outcome is a result of Australia&#x2019;s TRIANGLE in ASEAN program supporting safe and fair labour migration in the region.  The revised regulation is a significant step towards fairer treatment and improved livelihoods of domestic workers, predominantly women and migrants.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">TRIANGLE supported Cambodia&#x2019;s Ministry of Labour and Vocational Training (MoLVT) to prepare a Policy on Labour Migration (LMP) for Cambodia. The LMP builds on previous labour migration policies (2010-2014, 2015-2018, and 2019-2023), which were all developed with support from TRIANGLE.
&#x200d;
TRIANGLE supported two national consultation workshops in April and June 2025 to support the LMP&#x2019;s development. Over 125 stakeholders from MoLVT, other Cambodian ministries and government departments, trade unions, employer organisations, CSOs, and UN bodies took part in the consultation process. The LMP is expected to be endorsed by late 2025. The tripartite consultation approach which involved governments, employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organisation in the development and implementation of the LMP, along with ILO&#x2019;s support through the TRIANGLE program, were featured as good practice in the OECD Global Deal Flagship Report 2024.
&#x200d;
The LMP will be elevated from a ministerial to national-level policy and will cover a 10-year period (as opposed to four years under previous iterations).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening regional architecture</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The adoption of the ASEAN Vientiane Declaration on Skills Mobility, Recognition and Development for Migrant Workers and its Checklist at the ASEAN Summit in October 2024 was an important step forward for ASEAN in advancing skills recognition and realising middle-skilled migration within the region.
&#x200d;
Australia, through the TRIANGLE in ASEAN program, supported the development of the Vientiane Declaration in close coordination with Lao PDR as 2024 ASEAN Chair and the ASEAN Secretariat. TRIANGLE led the drafting of the Declaration and its checklist, and supported Lao PDR in hosting two consultation workshops to discuss and finalise the Declaration in April and June 2024 respectively.
&#x200d;
The Declaration supports a shift to labour migration patterns within ASEAN where migrant workers&#x2019; skills and qualifications are recognised as part of the recruitment process; where they have access to skill development and certification when working abroad; are paid wages according to their skill level; and where their skills and qualifications obtained abroad are recognised in their home countries on return.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
